    summary: "Injecting malicious JavaScript into web pages viewed by other users.",
    content: "# Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)\n\nXSS is a client-side code injection attack where an attacker injects malicious scripts into benign and trusted websites. XSS attacks occur when an attacker uses a web application to send malicious code, generally in the form of a browser-side script, to a different end user.\n\n## Three Main Types\n\n1. Stored (Persistent) XSS: payload is stored on the server (e.g., comment, profile field) and served to every visitor. Most dangerous because it can hit many users.\n2. Reflected (Non-Persistent) XSS: payload is embedded in a URL and reflected by the server in the response. Victim must click a crafted link.\n3. DOM-based XSS: payload executed entirely client-side via JavaScript sink functions without server involvement.\n\n## Common Payloads\n\nReflected example payload in a search parameter:\n\n    <script>alert(document.cookie)</script>\n\nDOM-based example where location.hash flows into innerHTML:\n\n    <script>document.getElementById('out').innerHTML = location.hash.slice(1)</script>\n\n## Dangerous Sinks\n\n- innerHTML, outerHTML, document.write\n- eval, Function, setTimeout(string), setInterval(string)\n- jQuery .html() and .append() with tainted input\n- href attribute of 'javascript:' URIs\n\n## Defense\n\n- Output encoding: escape HTML special characters (&, <, >, \", ') based on context (HTML body, attribute, JS, URL).\n- Use a Content Security Policy (CSP) header with 'nonce-' or 'strict-dynamic' and no 'unsafe-inline'.\n- Set the HttpOnly flag on session cookies so JS cannot read them.\n- Use modern frameworks (React, Vue) that auto-escape by default; avoid dangerouslySetInnerHTML / v-html.\n- Validate input on allowlists, not blocklists.\n\n## Tools\n\n- XSStrike: automated payload generation and WAF bypass.\n- OWASP ZAP: active scanner with XSS rules.\n- Browser DevTools: manual inspection of DOM sinks.",
    difficulty: "Beginner",
