    summary: "Overwriting memory beyond a buffer's bounds to hijack program control flow.",
    content: "# Buffer Overflow\n\nA buffer overflow occurs when a program writes more data to a buffer than it can hold, causing the excess data to overflow into adjacent memory. This can crash the program, corrupt data, or execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Stack-Based Overflow\n\nThe classic example: a C function uses strcpy to copy user input into a fixed-size stack buffer. strcpy does not check bounds, so input longer than the buffer overwrites the saved frame pointer and return address.\n\n    void vulnerable(char *input) {\n      char buf[64];\n      strcpy(buf, input);  // no bounds check\n    }\n\nWhen the function returns, the CPU pops the overwritten return address and jumps to it. If the attacker places shellcode on the stack and points the return address to it, the shellcode runs.\n\n## Heap-Based Overflow\n\nHeap overflows corrupt heap metadata or adjacent heap-allocated objects. Modern exploits use heap manipulation to gain code execution or to corrupt function pointers in objects (use-after-free, type confusion).\n\n## Mitigations\n\n- Stack canaries (cookies): compiler places a random value between local buffer and return address; if overwritten, the program aborts.\n- NX/DEP: marks the stack as non-executable so injected shellcode cannot run directly.\n- ASLR: randomizes the base address of stack, heap, libraries, and executable; attacker cannot hardcode addresses.\n- PIE: compiles the executable as position-independent so its base is also randomized.\n- RELRO: makes GOT read-only after dynamic linking to block GOT overwrite attacks.\n- Control-Flow Integrity (CFI): validates indirect branches against a whitelist of valid targets.\n- Use safe functions: strncpy/snprintf, strlcpy, std::string, Rust slices.\n\n## Modern Exploitation\n\nEven with all mitigations, exploits chain multiple primitives (information leak to defeat ASLR, ROP to defeat NX, leak canary via format-string or timing) to achieve code execution.",
