    summary: "Sending forged ARP replies to intercept or modify LAN traffic.",
    content: "# ARP Spoofing\n\nARP (Address Resolution Protocol) maps an IPv4 address to a MAC address on a local network. ARP is stateless and unauthenticated: any host can claim to own any IP by sending an unsolicited ARP reply. ARP Spoofing (also called ARP poisoning) exploits this to redirect traffic through an attacker-controlled host.\n\n## The Attack\n\nThe attacker continuously sends forged ARP replies to the victim: 'IP of the gateway is at MAC of attacker'. The victim updates its ARP cache and starts sending packets destined for the gateway to the attacker's MAC instead. The attacker does the same to the gateway for the victim's IP, placing itself as a man-in-the-middle.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Sniffing and modifying unencrypted traffic (HTTP, FTP, Telnet).\n- Session hijacking (stealing session cookies sent in cleartext).\n- Denial of Service by dropping forwarded packets.\n- SSL stripping: downgrade HTTPS links to HTTP (tools: sslstrip, bettercap).\n\n## Defense\n\n- Use end-to-end encryption (HTTPS, SSH, VPN). Even if traffic is intercepted, it cannot be read or modified.\n- Static ARP entries for critical hosts (gateway, servers); does not scale.\n- Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI) on managed switches: validates ARP packets against a DHCP snooping binding table.\n- Port security on switches: limit MACs per port.\n- 802.1X authentication: only authenticated devices can send traffic.\n- Network segmentation: keep untrusted networks (guest Wi-Fi) isolated.\n\n## Tools\n\n- arpspoof (dsniff suite), ettercap, bettercap.",
