    content: "# DNS Spoofing\n\nDNS Spoofing (DNS poisoning) is an attack where false DNS records are introduced into a resolver's cache, causing victims to be redirected to attacker-controlled IP addresses when resolving a domain.\n\n## Common Vectors\n\n1. Compromised local network (ARP spoofing + DNS packet injection): the attacker sniffs DNS queries and races the legitimate server with a forged response.\n2. Open recursive resolvers (Kaminsky attack): by flooding a vulnerable resolver with queries for random subdomains and racing fake responses, an attacker can poison the resolver's cache for the parent zone.\n3. Compromised DNS authoritative server or registrar account.\n4. Malware on the victim modifying the local hosts file or DNS resolver settings.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Phishing: victim thinks they visit bank.com but lands on a clone.\n- Credential theft: HTTPS with a valid cert would alert the user, but self-signed or downgraded connections can be exploited.\n- Drive-by malware distribution.\n\n## Defense\n\n- DNSSEC: cryptographically signs DNS records; the resolver validates the signature chain from the root zone. Prevents tampering with cached responses.\n- Use a trusted recursive resolver with DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) or DNS-over-TLS (DoT) to prevent in-path tampering.\n- Pin DNS for critical services with static entries where feasible.\n- Validate TLS certificates on the client side and do not bypass warnings.\n- Monitor for unexpected DNS responses on the network.\n\n## Tools\n\n- dnsspoof (dsniff), bettercap dns.spoof module, Responder (for LLMNR/NBT-NS poisoning on Windows networks).",
