    content: "# Burp Suite\n\nBurp Suite by PortSwigger is an integrated platform for performing security testing of web applications. The community edition is free; the professional and enterprise editions add automated scanning, advanced crawling, and reporting.\n\n## Core Components\n\n- Proxy: intercepts HTTP(S) traffic between the browser and the target. Supports on-the-fly editing, match-and-replace rules, and TLS pass-through.\n- Repeater: send individual requests repeatedly with manual tweaks; ideal for manual SQLi/XSS testing.\n- Intruder: brute-force payloads against a parameter (sniper, battering ram, pitchfork, cluster bomb).\n- Decoder: encode/decode base64, URL, hex, HTML entities, gzip.\n- Comparer: diff two responses visually.\n- Sequencer: analyze randomness of session tokens.\n- Scanner (Pro): automated crawler and vulnerability scanner.\n- Collaborator: out-of-band testing for blind vulnerabilities (SSRF, XXE, blind SQLi).\n\n## Typical Workflow\n\n1. Configure the browser to use Burp as HTTP/S proxy (default 127.0.0.1:8080).\n2. Install Burp's CA certificate so HTTPS traffic can be intercepted.\n3. Browse the target application to populate the Target site map.\n4. Send interesting requests to Repeater for manual probing.\n5. Use Intruder for fuzzing parameters or enumerating IDs.\n6. Right-click a request and 'Send to Scanner' (Pro) or run active scan.\n\n## Useful Extensions\n\n- Logger++ : detailed request/response logging.\n- Autorize: automated authorization testing.\n- JSON Beautifier, JWT Editor, Param Miner.\n\n## Tips\n\n- Use the Match-and-Replace feature to inject a header (e.g., X-Original-URL) on every request.\n- Use the 'Engagement tools' menu for finding comments, analyzing attack surface, and scheduling tasks.\n- Use Burp Collaborator payloads when testing for blind SSRF or XXE.\n\n## Ethics\n\nBurp's active scanner sends many attack payloads. Only run it against assets you are authorized to test.",
