    content: "# Wireshark\n\nWireshark is the world's most widely used network protocol analyzer. It lets you capture packets from a live interface or open saved capture files (pcap/pcapng) and inspect them at any protocol layer.\n\n## Capturing\n\n- Select a capture interface and start capture.\n- Use capture filters (BPF syntax) to limit what is captured, e.g., 'host 192.168.1.10 and port 80'.\n- Use display filters to slice a capture after the fact, e.g., 'http.request.method == POST' or 'tls.handshake.type == 1'.\n\n## Common Display Filters\n\n- ip.addr == 10.0.0.5\n- tcp.port == 443\n- http.request.uri contains \"login\"\n- dns.qry.name == \"evil.com\"\n- tls.record.content_type == 23  (application data)\n- frame contains \"password\"\n- !(arp or icmp or dns)  (noise reduction)\n\n## Useful Features\n\n- Follow TCP/HTTP/UDP stream: reassemble a conversation as raw text.\n- Statistics > Protocol Hierarchy: see traffic composition.\n- Statistics > Conversations: top talkers and byte counts.\n- Statistics > I/O Graphs: throughput over time.\n- Right-click a packet > 'Apply as Filter' or 'Prepare as Filter'.\n\n## TLS Decryption\n\nWireshark can decrypt TLS if you supply the session keys. Configure the client to log pre-master secrets via the SSLKEYLOGFILE environment variable, then point Wireshark at the file in Edit > Preferences > Protocols > TLS. This works for most browsers and curl.\n\n## Use Cases\n\n- Incident response: examine C2 beaconing and exfiltration patterns.\n- Application debugging: see exact bytes on the wire.\n- Compliance auditing: verify TLS configuration.\n- Forensics: timeline reconstruction from packet captures.\n\n## Command-line Sister\n\nFor headless servers, tshark provides the same protocol dissection. Example to list HTTP hosts requested:\n\n    tshark -r capture.pcapng -Y http.request -T fields -e http.host",
