    summary: "One-way functions for verifying data integrity and storing password hashes.",
    content: "# Hashing Algorithms\n\nA cryptographic hash function takes an input of arbitrary size and produces a fixed-size digest. Properties of a secure hash function are: preimage resistance, second-preimage resistance, and collision resistance.\n\n## Common Algorithms\n\n- MD5 (128-bit): broken for collision resistance since 2004. Do not use for security.\n- SHA-1 (160-bit): broken for collisions (SHAttered, 2017). Deprecated.\n- SHA-2 family: SHA-256, SHA-384, SHA-512. Recommended.\n- SHA-3 (Keccak): the latest standard, structurally different from SHA-2.\n- BLAKE2 / BLAKE3: very fast, parallelizable, used in modern systems.\n\n## Password Hashing\n\nPlain cryptographic hashes are too fast for storing user passwords; an attacker with a GPU can try billions per second. Use slow, salted password hashes:\n\n- bcrypt: tunable cost factor, includes salt, widely supported.\n- scrypt: memory-hard, resists ASIC/GPU brute force.\n- argon2id: winner of the Password Hashing Competition (2015); recommended default.\n- PBKDF2: NIST-approved, still common in legacy systems; use high iteration counts.\n\nEach password must have its own random salt (at least 16 bytes) so identical passwords do not produce identical hashes, and precomputed rainbow tables are useless.\n\n## Message Authentication Codes\n\nA MAC proves integrity and authenticity. HMAC combines a hash function with a secret key: HMAC-SHA256(key, message). Never reuse a single key as both an encryption key and an HMAC key.\n\n## Key Derivation\n\nDerive symmetric keys from passwords with HKDF or PBKDF2, never by hashing the password directly.\n\n## Identifying File Hashes\n\nOn Linux:\n\n    sha256sum file.iso\n    md5sum file.iso\n\nCompare against the publisher's checksum to verify integrity. For tamper-evidence, the checksum must be obtained through a trusted channel (HTTPS with valid cert, signed release file).",
