    id: "symmetric-encryption",
    title: "Symmetric Encryption",
    category: "Cryptography",
    tags: ["aes", "encryption", "key", "confidentiality"],
    summary: "Single-key encryption used for bulk data confidentiality.",
    content: "# Symmetric Encryption\n\nIn symmetric encryption the same key is used for both encryption and decryption. It is fast and suited for encrypting large amounts of data.\n\n## Block Ciphers\n\n- AES (Rijndael): the de facto standard, 128-bit blocks, key sizes 128/192/256 bits. Hardware-accelerated (AES-NI) on modern CPUs.\n- ChaCha20: stream cipher, often paired with Poly1305 MAC, fast on devices without AES hardware (mobile, IoT).\n- DES / 3DES: legacy, broken for new use; 3DES still seen in old banking systems.\n\n## Modes of Operation\n\nA block cipher only encrypts a single block. A mode of operation extends it to arbitrary-length messages.\n\n- ECB (Electronic Codebook): each block encrypted independently. Identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext; leaks patterns. Never use.\n- CBC (Cipher Block Chaining): each block XORed with previous ciphertext; needs an IV. Vulnerable to padding-oracle attacks if error handling leaks information.\n- CTR (Counter): encrypts a counter, XORs with plaintext. Effectively a stream cipher; requires unique nonce per key.\n- GCM (Galois/Counter Mode): CTR plus an authenticated tag (AEAD). Recommended default.\n- ChaCha20-Poly1305: AEAD stream cipher; recommended alternative to AES-GCM.\n\n## Authenticated Encryption (AEAD)\n\nAlways use AEAD so the ciphertext is also integrity-protected. AES-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305 are AEAD constructions. Without authentication, attackers can flip bits and observe behavior (padding oracles, MAC-then-encrypt pitfalls).\n\n## Key Rules\n\n- Never reuse a (key, nonce) pair in CTR/GCM. A single reuse breaks confidentiality.\n- Generate keys with a CSPRNG (e.g., /dev/urandom, crypto.getRandomValues).\n- Use established libraries: libsodium, Tink, Ring, OpenSSL EVP, NaCl. Do not roll your own.\n- Rotate keys periodically; protect them at rest with a KMS or HSM.\n\n## Common Library Usage\n\nWith libsodium (C) or its bindings:\n\n    crypto_aead_xchacha20poly1305_ietf_encrypt(c, &clen, m, mlen, ad, adlen, NULL, nonce, key);\n\nWith WebCrypto (browser):\n\n    crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, plaintext)",
