    summary: "Public-key cryptography for key exchange, signing, and authentication.",
    content: "# Asymmetric Encryption\n\nAsymmetric (public-key) cryptography uses a key pair: a public key that can be shared and a private key kept secret. Operations include encryption, signing, and key agreement.\n\n## RSA\n\nBased on the difficulty of factoring large integers. Common key sizes: 2048 bits (minimum), 3072 bits, 4096 bits. RSA is slow at encrypting data, so it is used to wrap a symmetric session key (hybrid encryption), not to encrypt bulk data.\n\nPadding matters:\n\n- PKCS#1 v1.5: vulnerable to Bleichenbacher padding oracle attacks in many implementations.\n- OAEP (Optimal Asymmetric Encryption Padding): recommended for new systems.\n- PSS (Probabilistic Signature Scheme): recommended for RSA signatures.\n\n## Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC)\n\nECC offers equivalent security to RSA with much smaller keys (256-bit ECC ~ 3072-bit RSA). Faster key generation and smaller signatures.\n\nCommon curves:\n\n- Curve25519 / Ed25519: modern, side-channel resistant, used in SSH, Signal, WireGuard.\n- NIST P-256 (secp256r1): widely deployed in TLS, but its generation is questioned.\n- secp256k1: used in Bitcoin and a few other cryptocurrencies.\n\n## Digital Signatures\n\nSign with the private key, verify with the public key. Provides authenticity, integrity, and non-repudiation.\n\n- RSA-PSS, RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5\n- ECDSA, Ed25519 (deterministic, fast, side-channel resistant)\n- The hash of the message is signed, not the message itself.\n\n## Key Agreement\n\nTwo parties derive a shared secret without transmitting it:\n\n- Diffie-Hellman (DH) over a finite field\n- ECDH (Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman), e.g., X25519\n- Used in TLS handshakes to establish a symmetric session key.\n\n## Practical Advice\n\n- Prefer Ed25519 keys for SSH ('ssh-keygen -t ed25519').\n- Use modern TLS 1.3 with AEAD and forward secrecy (ephemeral DH).\n- Rotate long-term keys; protect private keys with passphrase and hardware tokens (YubiKey, TPM).\n- Validate certificates chain up to a trusted root and check revocation (OCSP stapling preferred).",
